    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d410">

                <pb facs="0536" n="278" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg278"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1443</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Van Dyke</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 317]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">The Hague</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 24, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 6.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s instruction
                    No. 129 of June 29 enclosing a copy of a letter from Tomson and Company in which
                    it is alleged that the Netherlands Oversea Trust Company “only give their
                    assistance to Dutch concerns,” I have the honor to report that on several
                    occasions I have carefully investigated the status of the Netherlands Oversea
                    Trust with respect to issuing licenses for the importation of goods, American
                    and otherwise, for consumption in the Netherlands.</p>
                <p>I think the whole matter has been quite clearly reported in my despatches No. 299
                    of July 8 and No. 314 of July 19.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d410-f1"/>
                </p>
                <pb facs="0537" n="279" xml:id="pg279"/>
                <p>A full explanation of what has been called “discrimination” is found in my
                    despatch No. 299, in paragraph No IV.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry van Dyke</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
